            <ArticleTitle>Reliability and validity of the Korean Memorial Symptom Assessment Scale-Short Form in gynecological cancer patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0885-3924(17)30429-3</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jpainsymman.2017.08.023</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of the present study was to validate the Korean-version MSAS-SF in patients with gynecologic cancer.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We translated the MSAS-SF into Korean and 175 gynecologic cancer inpatients completed the MSAS-SF, Functional Assessment Cancer Therapy-General (FACT-G), and gathered demographic and clinical data and Karnofsky Performace Status (KPS). Reliability was assessed for internal consistency with Cronbach's alpha coefficient. Pearson's correlation coefficient was calculated between the MSAS-SF and FACT-G subscales for convergent validity. T-test analysis was used to compare differences in MSAS-SF subscales by cancer stage and KPS for discriminant validity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The Cronbach alpha coefficients for the MSAS-SF subscales ranged from 0.80 to 0.91. The Korean-version MSAS-SF subscales showed convergent validity with FACT-G subscales. The correlation coefficients were -0.640 (P &lt; 0.001) and -0.628 (P &lt; 0.001) for GDI (global distress index) and total MSAS score with FACT-G total score. The scores of MSAS-SF subscales showed appropriate differences by cancer stage and KPS.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The Korean-version MSAS-SF is a valid tool for the reliable assessment of patients with gynecologic cancer in Korea.</AbstractText>
